// Emit settings for the four browser bundles (app, quests, cards, admin).
//
// Until this branch, awesome-typescript-loader ran babel over its own ES6
// output (`awesomeTypescriptLoaderOptions.useBabel`), and package.json's babel
// section targeted `">2%", "last 2 years", "last 3 iOS versions",
// "chrome >= 30"` -- i.e. every shipped bundle was ES5. Dropping babel from the
// loader chain silently started shipping `target: es6` (classes, arrow
// functions, `const`/`let`, template literals) to those same browsers.
//
// services/app is packaged with Cordova: cordova-android@7.1 supports Android
// 4.4, whose WebView is Chrome 33, and cordova-ios@4.5 supports iOS 9. Neither
// can parse ES6 class syntax, so this is a hard white screen on those devices,
// not a graceful degradation. It also feeds ES6 to uglifyjs-webpack-plugin@1.
//
// This restores the previous emit target without touching `tsc --noEmit`
// (which stays on es6 -- it type-checks the node service too).
//
// `downlevelIteration` is what makes `for...of` over a non-array iterable emit
// correctly under es5 rather than as an index loop; every browser entrypoint
// already loads `babel-polyfill` first, which supplies `Symbol.iterator`.
//
// `lib` is pinned to what `target: es6` implied, so lowering the target does
// not also shrink the type surface if `transpileOnly` is ever turned off.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "es5",
    "downlevelIteration": true,
    "lib": ["dom", "dom.iterable", "es2015", "scripthost"]
  }
}
